From: MahanHD <mahanheidari2003@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] auto white balance: measure in the camera's colours

process_aaa() averages the processed preview, which has been through the
white balance gains, the colour matrix and the gamma, and it estimated
the camera's colour by dividing those averages by the gains alone. That
ignores the matrix and the gamma. Near the right gains the loop still
converged, but from a large error it overshot: on the Xperia Z1 Compact
the gains went 4.0, 0.99, 3.26, 1.21, 2.58 ... and took about twenty
steps, several seconds, to settle, and a photo taken meanwhile kept
whatever gains it had at the time.

Let the debayer undo its own processing instead. It remembers the gains
it last applied, takes the gamma off the averages and maps them back
through the inverse colour matrix, giving the frame's average colour as
the sensor saw it. Gains that make that grey are right in one step from
any starting point.

invert_matrix() had a typo in its determinant (in[4] * in[7] for
in[4] * in[6]); nothing called it before.

---
--- a/src/gles2_debayer.c	2026-10-16 10:36:49.434297500 +0400
+++ b/src/gles2_debayer.c	2026-10-16 10:37:43.336506378 +0400
@@ -4,6 +4,7 @@
